# Verification Integrity

`local-ci-before-push.mdc` says run the gates. This rule says know what they
assert. A check that never executed, or that cannot fail, is indistinguishable
from no check — and worse, because it gets reported as coverage.

## A documented step with no runnable command is a gap, not a step

If a runbook, CONTRIBUTING section, or checklist names a verification step and
gives no command, treat it as a missing gate: say so and name what is
unverified. Never restate a documented step as done.

`petralabx/skills` had a §5 titled "Validation before merge" that listed nothing
to run. Three defect classes reached agents behind it, and the manifest's
provenance pointer rotted unnoticed — see the 2026-07-26 entry in `LESSONS.md`.

## Prove a gate can fail before trusting it green

When adding a gate, or relying on an unfamiliar one for the first time, show it
failing on input known to be bad: replay it against the defect it was written
for, or seed the fault deliberately. Report that failure alongside the pass. A
gate only ever observed passing has been run, not tested.

## An environmentally-failing gate gets quarantined, not routed around

A check that fails for reasons unrelated to the change — platform line endings,
absent local credentials, clock skew — stops being a signal the moment its
failure becomes routine. Fix it, or quarantine it explicitly with a named owner
and a tracked task. "Known false positive" is not a disposition.

## Duplicated facts get a parity check or one source

When the same value must live in two places — generated surface and contract,
catalog pin and manifest, frontmatter and directory entry — add a check that
they agree, or collapse them to one source. This repo already uses that idiom
(`generate-governance-surfaces.py --check`, the design-system pin check); extend
it rather than relying on care.
